{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "rahab-rope",
  "text": "She ran a house on the city wall, lied to the king's face, and negotiated her whole family out of a war.\n\nJoshua two. Two Israelite spies come into Jericho and stay at the house of a woman named Rahab. The text says what she is. It doesn't soften it and neither will I.\n\nThe king finds out and sends men to bring them out.\n\nAnd Rahab hides the spies, then tells the king's messengers, \"There came men unto me, but I wist not whence they were.\" Then sends them chasing in the wrong direction.\n\nShe lied to a king. The Bible records it.\n\nThen she goes up to the roof and tells the spies the actual reason. \"I know that the Lord hath given you the land.\" She'd heard what happened at the Red Sea. She says, \"Our hearts did melt.\" And then, \"The Lord your God, he is God in heaven above, and in earth beneath.\"\n\nThat's a confession of faith from a Canaanite sex worker, and it's more theologically sharp than most of Israel manages that decade.\n\nThen she negotiates. And notice, not for herself. \"Save alive my father, and my mother, and my brethren, and my sisters, and all that they have.\"\n\nScarlet cord in the window. Her whole household lives.\n\nAnd Matthew puts her in the family line of Jesus.",
